I’m unable to make live calls to external services from this environment, so I can’t retrieve the actual data from VirusTotal for you.  
Below is a **sample structure** of what the response would look like if you queried the endpoint:

```json
{
  "data": [
    {
      "id": "subdomain1.example.org",
      "context_attributes": {
        "relationship": "subdomain",
        "first_seen": "2023-01-15T12:34:56Z",
        "last_seen": "2024-06-20T08:21:13Z"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "subdomain2.example.org",
      "context_attributes": {
        "relationship": "subdomain",
        "first_seen": "2022-11-02T09:12:45Z",
        "last_seen": "2024-05-30T14:55:00Z"
      }
    }
    /* … up to 10 items, truncated by the `limit` parameter */
  ],
  "links": {
    "next": "https://www.virustotal.com/api/v3/domains/example.org/related_objects?relationship=subdomains&limit=10&continuation_cursor=xyz"
  },
  "meta": {
    "continuation_cursor": "xyz"
  }
}
```

### How to actually perform the request

```http
GET https://www.virustotal.com/api/v3/domains/example.org/related_objects?relationship=subdomains&limit=10&continuation_cursor=abc
Authorization: Bearer my_api
Accept: application/json
```

Replace `my_api` with your real API key. The response will contain the IDs of the related subdomains and any context attributes that VirusTotal provides.
